comment.body:
Valid. Before the first passive effect, "not committed yet" and "unmounted" both left `committed` undefined, so a load settling before the first commit resolved at once. Fixed locally in `ca25ca852` (not pushed yet): the hook tracks `unmounted` separately, so a promise that settles before the first commit waits for it (or the 100ms timeout), and only an unmounted client resolves at once.

```ts
if (
  session.unmounted ||
  (committed !== undefined && Object.is(committed.state, getLatestState()))
) {
  resolve(value);
  return;
}
```

New `useAfterStateCommit.test.tsx` settles a promise during the first render outside `act()`, so it settles before the first passive effect. It fails without the fix. A second test checks that a promise still resolves at once after unmount.

@coderabbitai Took the test suggestion (committed locally, not pushed yet):

- The two "waits for act() to exit when the load settles inside it" tests are renamed to "holds the promise while the completing act() defers the commit", since they only check the first 20ms.
- New test in both `RemoteThreadList.load-promise.test.tsx` and `useRemoteThreadListRuntime.load-promise.test.tsx`:

```tsx
it("resolves inside the completing act() once the commit wait times out", async () => {
  const list = deferred<ListPage>();
  const adapter = makeAdapter({ list: vi.fn(() => list.promise) });
  const aui = mount(adapter);

  await act(async () => {
    list.resolve({
      threads: [{ remoteId: "t1", status: "regular", title: "One" }],
    });
    await aui.threads.getLoadThreadsPromise();
  });

  expect(aui.threads.getState().threadIds).toEqual(["t1"]);
});
```

Both new tests hang without the timeout fallback and pass with it (14/14 in the two files).

@coderabbitai Your retained Suspense concern is real. A `use()` reader of `reload()` inside the boundary that also holds the threads client hangs: the boundary hides the client, so it never commits the reloaded list the promise waits for. `main` doesn't hang in that case.

Proposed fix (local, not pushed yet): the delayed promise resolves after 100ms if the client hasn't committed, and the JSDoc says `getState()` may lag in that case.

```ts
const release = () => {
  clearTimeout(timeout);
  session.waiters.delete(release);
  resolve(value);
};
const timeout = setTimeout(release, COMMIT_TIMEOUT_MS);
session.waiters.add(release);
```

A new test, `resolves a reload read by use() inside the boundary that hides the client`, fails without this and passes with it, and the rest of the core suite passes (2752). Does this resolve the concern?

